1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,145
Here's how to reverse engineer Bitly from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,145 --> 00:00:07,980
Claude passed all nineteen checks, but my spec never said how to count the last thirty days.

3
00:00:08,883 --> 00:00:10,953
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:10,973 --> 00:00:13,641
It copies the part of Bitly a marketing team needs.

5
00:00:13,832 --> 00:00:15,369
Short links on your own domain.

6
00:00:15,411 --> 00:00:18,633
A UTM builder that only accepts your agreed sources and mediums.

7
00:00:18,653 --> 00:00:21,207
QR codes, counted separately as scans.

8
00:00:21,285 --> 00:00:24,848
And click counts that leave out bots and link previews.

9
00:00:25,783 --> 00:00:27,874
The core rule is counting.

10
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:31,053
A visitor is counted only once on a link in thirty seconds.

11
00:00:31,244 --> 00:00:35,937
Link previews from Slack or LinkedIn, and other bots, are recorded but never counted.

12
00:00:35,979 --> 00:00:40,033
A QR code carries a small marker in the link, so a scan counts apart from a click.

13
00:00:40,075 --> 00:00:42,480
And no IP address is ever stored.

14
00:00:43,417 --> 00:00:46,041
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

15
00:00:46,147 --> 00:00:47,919
The foundation and the link builder.

16
00:00:47,939 --> 00:00:49,497
Redirects and counting.

17
00:00:49,646 --> 00:00:51,161
Reports and exports.

18
00:00:51,353 --> 00:00:54,123
Then reminders, the weekly digest and safeguards.

19
00:00:54,205 --> 00:00:58,964
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

20
00:00:59,883 --> 00:01:00,929
Here's what it built.

21
00:01:01,078 --> 00:01:05,494
The Links screen shows five seed links, with three hundred and forty-two clicks on spring.

22
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:11,085
The new link form previews the final URL, with every UTM tag in order, and its QR code.

23
00:01:11,193 --> 00:01:14,799
The link page charts the clicks, with twenty-five previews left out.

24
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:18,746
The campaign report adds up four hundred and seventy clicks by source and medium.

25
00:01:18,873 --> 00:01:22,423
And the outbox holds the weekly digests and an expiry reminder.

26
00:01:23,350 --> 00:01:30,297
In one final run, on a fresh database, all nineteen checks passed, along with one hundred and fifty-one automated tests.

27
00:01:31,217 --> 00:01:33,394
But the more useful result is what broke.

28
00:01:33,479 --> 00:01:36,850
The test build exposed sixty-nine places where Claude had to guess.

29
00:01:36,935 --> 00:01:40,773
Sign-in was limited to the company email domain, but no setting held the domain.

30
00:01:40,813 --> 00:01:44,035
A one-time sign-in link could be used up by a link preview in Slack.

31
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:49,582
And the source and medium fields could be dropdowns, which made the check that refuses f b impossible to try.

32
00:01:49,688 --> 00:01:51,738
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

33
00:01:52,683 --> 00:01:55,286
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

34
00:01:55,413 --> 00:02:02,798
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, the daily job on a real scheduler, a real short domain, or redirect speed under load.

35
00:02:02,886 --> 00:02:07,088
And the QR codes were decoded by software, not scanned by a phone camera.

36
00:02:08,050 --> 00:02:09,458
So is it worth building?

37
00:02:09,671 --> 00:02:15,480
Billed yearly, Bitly's Core plan is ten dollars a month, Growth twenty-nine and Premium one hundred and ninety-nine.

38
00:02:15,528 --> 00:02:23,060
Changing where a link points is limited to five, twenty or one hundred and ten times a month, and multiple users are an Enterprise addition.

39
00:02:23,166 --> 00:02:26,610
If one person makes a handful of links a month, Core may be enough.

40
00:02:26,768 --> 00:02:31,400
If your team shares links and keeps changing where printed QR codes point, build it.

41
00:02:32,350 --> 00:02:33,908
Everything is in one file.

42
00:02:34,014 --> 00:02:40,478
The data model, nine rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all nineteen checks, with the sixty-nine fixes already in.

43
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:44,692
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

44
00:02:44,692 --> 00:02:46,762
If you build it, tell me what broke.

45
00:02:46,762 --> 00:02:47,222
I'm Sam.

46
00:02:47,222 --> 00:02:48,200
See you tomorrow.
